== what a C shim would pay per operation on the JVM (README 9.1's shape, priced before building it) ==

README section 9.1 is the Python binding's design: a generated C shim that speaks the
host runtime's C API instead of calling back into the host language, because "the C API
pays when it REMOVES a crossing, not when it replaces one". The JVM analogue is a shim
that reads and writes facade fields through the JNI API instead of upcalling into Java,
and it is aimed at this slice's decode regression: 7.004 reverse calls per element on
P2.2 at about 80 ns each is 560 ns of a 1.441 ratio.

Whether it can win is arithmetic, so this prices both sides of it before the arm exists.
Each op is looped INSIDE one native call and reported net of an empty-loop control at the
same count, because a shim is already in C when it pays these. Three collectors, because
the answer turned out to depend on the write barrier.

The `k` table is the comparison that matters. `upcall` is what the binding does today:
one cached reverse call, k straight-line stores in bytecode inside it. `shim` is k
SetObjectField calls and no reverse call. Both write k DISTINCT values into k DISTINCT
fields -- the first cut of this probe had the callee storing one value into one field k
times, which C2 reduces to a single store, and that defective control sat in the arm that
decides whether to build the shim at all.

